onvenience does not relieve customer of fees accrued through the effective date.”\n\nPlain-English reading (preserve actors & terms)\n- If the Customer (you) elects to “terminate for convenience” by giving written notice, §3.2 requires 90 days’ notice; the termination’s effective date will be the date the termination becomes effective under that notice (in ordinary reading, 90 days after notice unless the contract defines “effective date” differently).\n- Under §7.1, terminating for convenience does not relieve the Customer of “fees accrued through the effective date.” That means the Customer remains liable for all fees that have been incurred/earned up to the termination effective date. In practice that includes:\n  - fees already incurred before you give notice (outstanding/unpaid invoices), and\n  - fees that accrue during the 90‑day notice period up to the effective date (unless another clause says otherwise).\n\nHow to quote both pieces in your memo (suggested, exact quoting)\n- “Per MSA §3.2 (p.4): ‘either party may terminate for convenience upon ninety days written notice.’”\n- “Per MSA §7.1 (p.11): ‘termination for convenience does not relieve customer of fees accrued through the effective date.’”\n\nPractical points to confirm in the PDF before finalizing the memo\n- Whether the contract contains any specific definition of “effective date” or “accrued fees” (those definitions can change the interpretation).\n- Any other termination-related provisions (e.g., minimum commitment/early‑termination fees, liquidated damages, refunds of prepayments, SOW-specific termination, invoicing/cutoff rules) that could alter how much you owe.\n- Any cure, offset, or setoff rights the Customer may have.\n\nIf you want, paste the definitions section or any other termination/fees clauses and I’ll check them against §3.2 and §7.1.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_0ded626016b57fc7016a9a0939dd4487d09ba726deb1cdb86a","role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message"}],"par